```
    r = git(tree, "-c", "core.quotepath=off", "diff", "--name-status", "-M", since_sha, tip)
    if not r.ok:
        return {}
```
The docstring fixes the meaning of the return value: "A path not in the result is where it was." On any failure (`since_sha` garbage-collected after a `gc`/shallow clone, `since_sha` from a different history than `tip`, an unreadable object, a pathspec/rev error) the function returns `{}`, which is byte-identical to "nothing was renamed or deleted". So the failure is reported as a *pass*: a consumer such as PL-staleness/RR-freshness ("paths missing/moved", "files renamed/deleted") concludes the item's paths are all still in place when git never answered. This contradicts the module's own contract stated in its header — `"Could not tell" is behind=None, never 0` — which `measure` honours (returns `behind=None`) and `moved_or_gone`, `_rules`'s caller path excepted, does not; callers have no way to distinguish "no moves" from "unknown". Returning a sentinel (or `None`) on `not r.ok` would keep the two apart.

